      "answer": "$c>11$",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$x^2-6x+c>2$, leading to $(x-3)^2-9+c>2$ — M1A1; M1 for completing the square in an equation or inequality that retains the 2.\n$c>11-(x-3)^2$, together with $(x-3)^2\\ge0$ — M1; shown or implied through the minimum value.\n$c>11$ — A1.\nAlternative differentiation: $dy/dx=2x-6=0$ — M1; $x=3$ — A1; at $x=3$, $y=c-9$ — M1; $c-9>2$, so $c>11$ — A1.\nAlternative discriminant: write $x^2-6x+c-2>0$ and apply the discriminant condition — M1; $36-4(c-2)<0$ — M1A1, requiring the correct inequality; hence $c>11$ — A1.\nTotal: 4",
